<%#
  "About these ratings", on /elo-chart and /player-stats. The ELO part is for everyone; the
  points rating and its division bands are a superadmin's only (/admin/team-strength).
    include('ratings-explainer.ejs', { showPoints, bands })   bands: TeamStrength.divisionBands()
%>
<details class="card mb-3 ratings-explainer">
  <summary class="card-header" style="cursor:pointer;">About these ratings</summary>
  <div class="card-body small text-left">
    <h2 class="h6">ELO rating</h2>
    <p>Everyone starts at 1500. After every game the winning pair gains points and the losing pair
      loses the same number: more for beating a stronger pair, fewer for beating a weaker one. A new
      player's rating moves faster for their first 15 games, a wide winning margin counts a little more
      than a narrow one, and partners of different ratings share the change according to the gap.
      Playing a team from a higher division counts as being the underdog. Ratings carry on from one
      season to the next.</p>
    <p>Each game only moves points between the four players in it, and most games are played within a
      division, so each division's ratings mostly stay in their own pool. A strong Division 3 player can
      out-rate a Premier one: the ELO says how someone is doing against the players they meet, not where
      they would stand across the whole league.</p>
    <% if (showPoints) { %>
    <h2 class="h6 mt-3">Points rating <span class="badge badge-secondary">admin only</span></h2>
    <p>Worked out every night from every game since 2018, plus the Tameside and Manchester league games of
      players who also play there (matched by name). It works rally by rally, so a 21–15 win is 36 rallies,
      not just one result. It finds the set of strengths that best explains every score at once, rather than
      adding and subtracting game by game. A recent game counts more than an old one: a game a year old
      counts half. A player with only a few games starts from the average of their division.</p>
    <p>Because reserves playing up, promoted teams and players changing club all link one division to
      another, it compares players across divisions directly, and the ratings fall into bands by division
      (below). A pair 100 points stronger than their opponents wins about 52.5% of rallies, which is about
      63% of games. Tested on the last two seasons, it picked the winner of 75% of games against the ELO's 71%.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Compare men with men, and ladies with ladies.</strong> Mixed games compare a man only with
        other men and a lady only with other ladies, so the two scales are separate. A man and a lady on the
        same number are not equal.</li>
      <li><strong>± is how sure it is.</strong> The shading on the charts. It is wide for a player with few
        recent games, and one good or bad night can move them a long way.</li>
      <li><strong>On the charts it is weekly.</strong> Each Monday's point is worked out from the games
        before that Monday only, so a past week never changes with hindsight.</li>
    </ul>
    <% if (bands && bands.length) { %>
    <p class="mb-1">Where this season's registered players stand now. The middle 80% of each division lies
      between the low and high figures; the bands overlap, but not by much.</p>
    <div class="table-responsive">
    <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-0" style="max-width:40rem;">
      <thead><tr><th>Division</th><th>Gender</th><th class="text-right">Players</th><th class="text-right">Low (10%)</th><th class="text-right">Middle</th><th class="text-right">High (90%)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <% bands.forEach(function(b) { %>
        <tr><td><%= b.division %></td><td><%= b.gender === 'Female' ? 'Ladies' : 'Men' %></td><td class="text-right"><%= b.players %></td>
          <td class="text-right"><%= b.p10 %></td><td class="text-right"><strong><%= b.median %></strong></td><td class="text-right"><%= b.p90 %></td></tr>
      <% }) %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <% } %>
    <% } %>
  </div>
</details>
